course: dictionary
lesson: 14133
title: The word minimisation
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Minimisation comes from the verb minimise, which means to make something as small as possible. The ending -ation turns the action into a noun, so minimisation names the act or process of making something smaller. «The school started a plan for waste minimisation» means the school wanted to make less rubbish.
explain: In maths, minimisation means finding the smallest possible value of something, such as the smallest cost, distance or time, while following some rules. Think of a map: a minimisation problem asks which route from home to school is the shortest.
explain: Minimisation can also mean making a problem seem less serious than it really is. If someone is hurt and you say «it's nothing», you are minimising the problem. That can stop them getting help, so it is harmful. Good readers notice when a word like this changes how a sentence feels.
fact: Minimisation is a noun, so it names an action or a process.
fact: The verb is minimise. The British spelling is minimisation; the US spelling is minimization.
fact: «Minimisation» can mean making something as small as possible, as in «the minimisation of waste».
fact: In maths, minimisation means finding the smallest possible cost, distance or time under some rules.
fact: «Risk minimisation» means reducing the chance of harm. Minimising a real problem, by saying it is nothing, can stop people getting help.
exercise: «The class worked on waste minimisation all week.» What does minimisation mean here? | *Making rubbish as small as possible | Counting the bins | Making a bigger mess | Painting the walls
exercise: In maths, minimisation means finding what? | A colourful picture | The biggest possible value | *The smallest possible value under some rules | The number of people in a room
exercise: «Saying "it's nothing" when Sam is hurt is minimisation.» What does this mean? | Telling Sam a joke to cheer him up | *Making a real problem seem less serious than it is | Giving Sam a big hug | Making a small cut into a big injury
test: «Risk minimisation» is about making what smaller? | The number of books | *The chance of harm | The length of the lunch break | The size of the classroom
test: Which words show minimisation of a problem? | «We are calling the nurse now» | «I'll stay with you until it is better» | «Let's get some help» | *«It's only a scratch, you'll be fine» when a child is badly hurt
exam: Which sentence shows waste minimisation? | «The factory held a big party.» | «The factory bought more lorries.» | *«The factory changed its packaging so it made as little waste as possible.» | «The factory closed for the holidays.»
